#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b8b10a8-f81a-5dae-b54e-b10959e3ad14" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14383" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $a_n = 1-2n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + d, \\qquad a_1 = \\_\\_\\_ \\,, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 1 - 2(1) =-1 \\\\\na_{2} &= 1 - 2(2) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&=-3 - (-1) \\\\\n&=-3 + 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}-2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1}=a_n{\\color{red}-2}, \\qquad a_1={\\color{blue}-1}, \\qquad n\\geq1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d36a3a61-e23b-5ce3-ac65-f7ac1691ec4e" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Explicit Formula to a Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14386" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $f(n) = 3n-11$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + d, \\qquad f(1) = \\_\\_\\_ \\,, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 3(1) - 11 =-8 \\\\\nf(2) &= 3(2) - 11 =-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= f(2) - f(1) \\\\\n&=-5 - (-8) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}-8}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad f(1) = {\\color{blue}-8}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" :question/math-academy-id "e-14389" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence\n$a_{n+1} = a_n + 3,\\, \\qquad a_1 = -1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1 = {\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + 3 \\\\\n&=-1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\na_1 = {\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1+(n-1)d \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}-1} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}3}) \\\\[5pt] & =-1 + 3n - 3 \\\\[5pt] &=3n - 4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3n - 4, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baac268a-629e-5887-885a-49393c72776f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" :question/math-academy-id "e-14393" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) = f(n) - 5,\\, \\qquad f(1) = 3,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n) = f(1) + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= f(1) - 5 \\\\\n&= 3 - 5 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= f(2) - f(1) \\\\\n&=-2 - 3 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\nf(1) = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}-5}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n)&= f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}3} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-5}) \\\\[5pt] & =3 - 5n + 5 \\\\[5pt] &=8 - 5n \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=8 - 5n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$"}]}
